<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tudhaliya IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1237–1209 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tudhaliya IV was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> in the thirteenth century BCE, one of its last significant rulers. His reign saw extensive religious reorganisation and building, but also mounting pressures on the empire. Accounts of the Hittite Empire list succession conflicts after his death among the factors, along with drought, famine and the fragmentation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> network, that contributed to the empire's collapse.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tudhaliya-iv.jpg" alt="Tudhaliya IV" width="289" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: China_Crisis · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yazilikaya.TudhaliyaIV.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tudḫaliya IV was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a>, the younger son of Ḫattušili III and the great queen Puduḫepa. He was probably born at his father's court in Ḫattuša, after his brother Nerikkaili, the crown prince, while their father was still governing on behalf of his own brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muwatalli-ii">Muwatalli II</a>. Tudḫaliya was a good friend of Muwatalli's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurunta">Kurunta</a>, and Ḫattušili ordered that the two stay on good terms.</p><p>According to the document known as the Apology or Autobiography of Ḫattušili III, the future king named him priest of the goddess Šauška of Samuha. After Ḫattušili, now king, drew up a treaty confirming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurunta">Kurunta</a>'s rule over Tarḫuntašša, he raised Tudḫaliya above his older brother to be crown prince. His sister Maathorneferure served as Great Royal Wife to Pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The empire covered large parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Drier conditions brought a severe drought, and Tudḫaliya IV responded by building at least 13 dams to secure water supplies, one of which still survives at Alacahöyük. To the east he faced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> and suffered a severe defeat at the hands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tukulti-ninurta-i">Tukulti-Ninurta I</a> in the Battle of Nihriya. In a treaty with his vassal Šaušgamuwa of Amurru he named the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, Karanduniyaš, which is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, and Assyria, and possibly Aḫḫiyawa, as equal to himself, and ordered that Šaušgamuwa treat the Assyrian king as an enemy and bar his own merchants from trade with Assyria.</p><p>The wording hints at an alliance or tacit understanding with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. In the south-west the empire had been divided, with parts of the territory under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurunta">Kurunta</a> of Tarḫuntašša, and Tudḫaliya drew up a treaty, recorded on a bronze tablet, confirming his links with Kurunta. A clay tablet from the reign of Šuppiluliuma II says Tudḫaliya set up a statue to commemorate his conquest of Alasiya, possibly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. He had two sons, Arnuwanda III and Šuppiluliuma II, who are considered the last two kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tudḫaliya IV” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Other factors were succession conflicts around the reign of <b>Tudhaliya IV</b>, prolonged drought and famine documented in correspondence requesting emergency grain shipments from Egypt, and the fragmentation of the vassal network that had sustained imperial revenues.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In a treaty of <b>Tudhaliya IV</b> the king of Ahhiyawa was written among the great kings of the age and then struck out.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>In a treaty of <b>Tudhaliya IV</b>, c. 1230 BCE, the king of Ahhiyawa was struck from the list of great kings, suggesting its power had waned.</blockquote><a href="/state/ahhiyawa">Ahhiyawa</a></li><li><blockquote>The so-called Milawata letter, probably from <b>Tudhaliya IV</b>, shows the town had passed under Hittite control and a new border was being fixed.</blockquote><a href="/state/millawanda">Millawanda</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Tudhaliya IV</b> a king backed by Ahhiyawa rebelled and was defeated.</blockquote><a href="/state/seha-river-land">Seha River Land</a></li><li><blockquote>A treaty between <b>Tudhaliya IV</b> and Kurunta, on a bronze tablet found at Hattusa in 1986, defines its borders and privileges.</blockquote><a href="/state/tarhuntassa">Tarhuntassa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nerik">Nerik</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.21,35.42&amp;year=-1223" aria-label="Nerik on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Tudhaliya IV"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5348 4039h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5348 4039h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.21,35.42&amp;year=-1223">What was here in 1223 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
